    "text": "[Status Effect: Minor Hemorrhage applied. Duration: 12s. HP -2/sec.]\n\nThe math was always the worst part of bleeding out. Twelve seconds meant twenty-four hit points. With a current HP pool sitting at a miserable thirty-one, survival wasn\u2019t a matter of willpower or grit; it was a basic arithmetic problem. \n\nA hand clamped down over the jagged tear in the leather armor, applying physical pressure. It didn't stop the System from ticking down the damage, but it made the meat underneath feel slightly less exposed to the drafty, copper-scented air of the cavern. \n\n[9s. HP: 25/140]\n\nA ragged breath hissed through clenched teeth. Just wait out the timer. \n\n[3s. HP: 13/140]\n\n[Status Effect: Minor Hemorrhage removed.]\n\nA heavy sigh echoed off the damp stone. Surviving by eleven hit points felt less like a hard-fought victory and more like a clerical error. But the System, in its infinite, emotionally dead wisdom, decided it was enough to trigger the payout.\n\n[Experience Threshold Met. Level 14 -> 15.]\n[+5 Free Attribute Points available.]\n\nThere it was. The dopamine hit. The Pavlovian bell that made the stench of viscera and the lingering phantom pain of a severed artery entirely worth it. Five points. Five tiny, numerical weights to tip the scales of survival. Dumping them into Vitality was the smart play\u2014the coward\u2019s play. Dumping them into Agility meant maybe not getting stabbed in the first place. \n\nThe interface hung in the air, a crisp, glowing blue rectangle that cared nothing for the shivering, bruised mess of a human reading it. It was clean. Perfect. Unyielding.\n\nThen, the blue text flickered. The sharp edges of the notification window blurred, bleeding into a bruised, sickly purple. \n\n[Anomaly Detected. System Overlay Integrity: 88%.]\n[Hidden Milestone Reached: The Weight of Eyes.]\n[Warning: Do not assign points in the dark. They can see the math.]\n\nThe rush of the level-up vanished, instantly replaced by a cold, leaden dread pooling in the gut. The numbers were going up, sure. The progression was real. But the math was starting to look an awful lot like a trap.",
